# Steam Deck packages CARRIED IN THE OFFLINE MIRROR.
#
# These are added to build-iso.sh's `all_packages`, so `pacman -Syw` downloads
# them into var/cache/omarchy/mirror/offline/ and prune-offline-mirror.sh keeps
# them.
#
# 🔴 THIS FILE INSTALLS NOTHING. IT ONLY DECIDES WHICH BUILD IS CARRIED.
#
# The header that stood here until 2026-08-15 said "The omarchy-deck package
# (T5-fork-plan §3 seam S4) declares them and pulls them out of the mirror
# during `configure_deck`." **That was never implemented.** `omarchy-deck`
# declares no such dependencies, `configure_deck` had no step that installed
# anything, and the whole list was therefore inert: the ISO downloaded a
# 350 MiB Neptune firmware package, an entire Valve kernel and Valve's
# gamescope, shipped them uncompressed inside the squashfs, and installed none
# of them. Measured on the first hardware boot ever produced by this project --
# docs/findings/P32-steam-never-installed.md. The offline mirror is also GONE
# after the install (it is bind-mounted for pacstrap and unmounted before
# genfstab), so "installed later" had no later to happen in. (That 350 MiB
# firmware package is GONE as of 2026-08-15 -- see the removal note below. The
# kernel and gamescope now have real installers.)
#
# THE RULE NOW, and every line below states which half it is:
#
#   here ONLY               carried and installed by NOBODY. Legitimate only
#                           for something whose reader is named in the entry's
#                           own comment (today: the -headers line, and ONLY that
#                           line, whose reader is "nothing, deliberately").
#   here AND, bare, in      the DUAL-ENTRY pattern. This file picks the build
#   deck-install.packages   (and may repo-qualify to do it); that file makes
#                           pacstrap install it, offline, on the target.
#
# Why entries live here at all rather than only in deck-install.packages:
#
#  * Repo-qualified names are legal here and illegal in deck-install.packages --
#    this list is only ever resolved against the online repos, that one is
#    resolved twice against the single-repo offline mirror.
#  * build-iso.sh strips the BARE form of every qualified entry here out of the
#    online download list, so a `foo` in the install list and a `repo/foo` here
#    do not reach `pacman -Syw` as a duplicate target. An identical bare/bare
#    pair needs no stripping: `all_packages` is `sort -u`'d.
#
# ⚠️ Measured cost, 2026-08-12: adding this file plus deck-install.packages took
# the mirror from 1241 to 1283 package files, +544 MiB compressed. The offline
# mirror is stored UNCOMPRESSED inside the squashfs on purpose
# (configs/profiledef.sh's `uncompressed@subpathname(...)`), so that is ~1:1 on
# ISO size. T5g owns the size gate; these are the numbers it starts from.
#
# `linux-firmware-neptune` was 349.6 MiB of that +544 and was CUT on 2026-08-15
# (P33/F1, hardware evidence in the removal note below), leaving ~+194 MiB.
# PREDICTED ISO: the build this was measured against is 6,854,164,480 B
# (6.383 GiB); without the firmware it should land near 6,487,552,126 B
# (6.042 GiB). That is arithmetic at the documented ~1:1 ratio, NOT a measured
# build -- squashfs block alignment and the offline repo db will move it a
# little. Re-measure after the next build rather than quoting this line.

# --- retired kernel slot (jupiter-staging; Valve-only, no name overlap) -----
#
# 🔴 RETIRED 2026-09-17 (v4.0.4 rebase): `linux-neptune-611` lived here (and
# in deck-install.packages) as a DUAL ENTRY. Operator decision adopts
# upstream's generic `linux-omarchy`, which arrives via the runtime's own
# `install/omarchy-other.packages` -- no mirror line of ours is needed, and
# carrying 350+ MiB of kernel nobody installs is the P32 defect, repeated.
# Recorded so the next reader does not "restore" it.

# ❌ REMOVED 2026-08-15 (P33/F1): `linux-firmware-neptune`, 366,612,354 B /
# 349.6 MiB. Recorded here so the next reader does not "restore" it.
#
# It never had a reader. Pacstrapping it kills the install at phase 3 on file
# conflicts with Arch's split linux-firmware (that is why deck-install.packages
# refuses it, and that refusal STANDS -- do not add it there either); the only
# code that can swap it is src/omarchy-deck-kernel.sh's `pacman -Rdd` dance,
# which runs on a booted system against the ONLINE Valve repos and so never read
# this mirror copy. The mirror is also unmounted before genfstab, so "installed
# later, from here" had no later to happen in.
#
# It was held anyway until hardware could answer, because
# docs/findings/P32-neptune-firmware-placement.md is static analysis: it swept
# all 6,150 modules of linux-neptune-611 for `firmware=` declarations (1,973
# distinct files) and found only 32 that Arch's firmware lacks, every one for
# hardware the Deck does not have (AceNIC/Cassini NICs, ADSL and dial-up modems,
# a DVB tuner, a webcam). Wi-Fi/BT blobs byte-identical or same-QC_IMAGE_VERSION;
# Arch's vangogh ucode newer-or-equal in all 11 files. But `linux-neptune-611` +
# Arch firmware had never actually booted on the Deck.
#
# IT HAS NOW. Read off the operator's Deck over SSH, 2026-08-15, after it was
# reinstalled from our own ISO:
#
#   uname -r                        6.11.11-valve29-1-neptune-611-g2dcfaf4df7ac
#   pacman -Q linux-firmware-neptune   error: package ... was not found
#   installed firmware              linux-firmware{,-amdgpu,-atheros,-cirrus,
#                                   -radeon,-whence,...} 20260810-1  (Arch's split set)
#   ath11k_pci 0000:03:00.0         qca2066 hw2.1, fw_build_id
#                                   WLAN.HSP.1.1-03926.13-QCAHSPSWPL_V2_SILICONZ_CE-2.52297.9
#   `journalctl -k | grep -i "firmware.*(fail|not found|direct load)"`   NO MATCHES
#
# Wi-Fi carried that SSH session over wlo1; panel, audio and gamescope/Gaming
# Mode all confirmed working by the operator. The ath11k fw_build_id is the same
# QC_IMAGE_VERSION_STRING the sweep read out of Arch's amss.bin -- the static
# claim and the running radio agree. That is exactly the test §5.33b named.
#
# ⚠️ This is now a REGRESSION CHECK, not a discovery: any future hardware run
# must still show Wi-Fi, audio and gamescope up without Valve's firmware.

# 🔴 RETIRED 2026-09-17 (v4.0.4 rebase): `linux-neptune-611-headers` lived
# here, mirror-only, reader-less. Upstream now guarantees matching headers
# for its own kernels (migration 1789444024 installs `linux-omarchy-headers`
# whenever `linux-omarchy` is present), so a Valve-headers line has no reader
# today and no reader after the rebase. This executes the cut P33/F1 already
# recommended. Recorded so the next reader does not "restore" it.

# --- Valve's audio DSP firmware ----------------------------------------------
#
# 🔴 REPO-QUALIFIED, and DUAL ENTRY with the bare `steamdeck-dsp` in
# deck-install.packages -- exactly the gamescope pattern below. Qualifying is
# what makes the build deterministic if Arch ever publishes a name collision,
# and it makes build-iso.sh strip the bare form out of the online download list
# so `pacman -Syw` gets one unambiguous target instead of two.
#
# It was in deck-fetch.packages until 2026-08-15 (T5-fork-plan.md §4.1 chose
# fetch-not-bundle on redistribution grounds). Operator decision 2026-08-15
# reversed that on the grounds that it is FIRMWARE the machine needs to have
# working audio at all. `steam` did NOT move and is still fetched.
#
# Verified against the live repo 2026-08-15: jupiter-staging carries
# steamdeck-dsp-0.38-1-any.pkg.tar.zst; holo-staging is configured but carries
# nothing this build names.
jupiter-staging/steamdeck-dsp

# --- Gaming Mode --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 🔴 REPO-QUALIFIED DELIBERATELY. `gamescope` exists in both extra (3.16.25-1)
# and jupiter-staging (3.16.25-3), pacman resolves `-S <name>` by REPO ORDER and
# not by version, and the Valve repos are appended last -- so a bare `gamescope`
# entry silently fetches Arch's bare compositor instead of Valve's build, which
# is the one that ships the SteamOS session. docs/PROGRESS.md §5.13 measured
# this and chose exactly this fix ("qualify the package, do not reorder repos")
# over putting Valve's repos first, which would have downgraded 50 packages.
#
# Verified 2026-08-12: with the qualification, the resolved keep-set draws
# exactly 4 packages from jupiter-staging -- this one and the three above --
# and nothing else leaks in from Valve's mirror. (That count is now 5:
# jupiter-staging/steamdeck-dsp joined them 2026-08-15. Re-measure rather than
# trusting either number after the next build.)
#
# DUAL ENTRY with the bare `gamescope` in deck-install.packages -- and it has
# been since 2026-08-15, which is why gamescope is the ONE thing from this file
# that reached the hardware. It is the template the rest of this list was
# rebuilt on.
jupiter-staging/gamescope

# --- HUD ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NOT qualified, deliberately: §5.13's follow-on found Arch's mangohud 0.8.4-1
# is NEWER than Valve's 0.8.3-4 and already ships /usr/bin/mangoapp, verified on
# the Deck. Arch wins here because the Valve repos are last, which is what we
# want -- do not "fix" these into jupiter-staging/ to match the line above.
#
# DUAL ENTRY as of 2026-08-15: the bare names are in deck-install.packages, so
# pacstrap installs them. Until then they were carried and installed by nobody,
# which meant Gaming Mode's performance overlay could never work --
# src/deck-session.sh warns "mangoapp not found (install mangohud)" and
# docs/PROGRESS.md records that the session references mangoapp but no package
# pulls it. Bare in both files, so `sort -u` collapses the pair.
mangohud
lib32-mangohud
